#---
# purpose: "atom-keyed map iteration uses the same name order as the language term order"
#---
#
# The allocation counts are part of the ordering contract. Comparing atom
# names must not materialize fz strings. Construction allocates the one
# 64-byte source map, written in key order, on every path. That allocation is
# classified as Map, not Other; total allocation stays fixed: map `1/64`,
# list cons `15/240`, struct `3/96`, scalar box `0/0`. An atom ref carries
# its id and allocates nothing, and the integer `Kernel.compare/2` answers
# reaches `dbg/1` as a word and its kind, so nothing is boxed.
#
# Other is `0/0`. The total is `29/688` on the interpreter. The native paths add
# the continuation frames the program builds, 15 frames of 784 bytes, and the
# two closures the scheduler builds to start the program (64 bytes), and report
# `46/1536`.
# Dynamic list heads use the same source-retention guard as statically known heads.
# Each pair is `allocs/bytes`. The fixed atom-list input to
# `Enum.sort/1` is deliberate: feeding it `Map.keys/1` would change merge-sort's
# work-list shape when the bug is fixed and would confound comparator cost with
# a different input order.
def main() do
  map = %{z: 1, a: 2, m: 3}

  dbg(Kernel.compare(:z, :a))
  dbg(Enum.sort([:z, :a, :m]))
  dbg(Map.keys(map))
  dbg(Map.values(map))
  dbg(Map.to_list(map))

  stats = System.alloc_stats()
  dbg({
    stats[:map_allocs],
    stats[:map_bytes],
    stats[:list_cons_allocs],
    stats[:list_cons_bytes],
    stats[:struct_allocs],
    stats[:struct_bytes],
    stats[:scalar_box_allocs],
    stats[:scalar_box_bytes],
    stats[:other_allocs],
    stats[:other_bytes],
    stats[:allocs],
    stats[:bytes]
  })
end
